# Implementation Plan Formatting Guide

## Overview
This rule provides specific guidance on how to format and present implementation plans as deliverable documents. Follow these formatting conventions to ensure clarity, readability, and actionability.

## Document Structure

### 1. Title and Metadata
```
# [Project/Feature Name] Implementation Plan
- **Version**: [1.0]
- **Last Updated**: [YYYY-MM-DD]
- **Status**: [Draft/In Review/Approved]
- **Owner**: [Name/Role]
- **Estimated Timeline**: [Start Date] to [End Date]
```

### 2. Table of Contents
- Include a detailed table of contents with clickable links
- Use hierarchical numbering for easy reference (1, 1.1, 1.1.1)

### 3. Standard Sections
Organize the document with these standard sections:
1. Executive Summary
2. Background and Context
3. Technical Approach
4. Implementation Phases
5. Detailed Action Items
6. Testing Strategy
7. Rollout Plan
8. Risk Assessment
9. Appendices (if needed)

## Formatting Standards

### Headings
- Use Markdown heading levels consistently
- Main sections: H1 (#)
- Subsections: H2 (##)
- Components within subsections: H3 (###)
- Specific items: H4 (####)

### Lists and Tables
- Use bulleted lists for non-sequential items
- Use numbered lists for sequential steps or prioritized items
- Use tables for comparing options or presenting structured data:

```markdown
| Component | Technology | Justification | Alternatives Considered |
|-----------|------------|---------------|-------------------------|
| Frontend  | React      | Team expertise| Vue, Angular            |
```

### Code Blocks
- Use syntax-highlighted code blocks for examples
- Include comments in code examples
- Keep examples concise but complete

```typescript
// Example TypeScript interface
interface UserData {
  id: string;         // Unique identifier
  name: string;       // Full name
  email: string;      // Contact email
  role: UserRole;     // Access control role
}
```

### Diagrams
- Include relevant diagrams (architecture, flow, sequence)
- Use descriptions for each diagram
- Reference diagrams in the text

### Action Items
Format action items with clear ownership and status:

```markdown
#### Action Item 1.1: [Title]
- **Description**: Detailed explanation of the task
- **Owner**: [Role/Team]
- **Dependencies**: [Action Items this depends on]
- **Estimated Effort**: [Low/Medium/High] or [X person-days]
- **Acceptance Criteria**: List of criteria to consider this complete
- **Status**: [Not Started/In Progress/Completed]
```

## Presentation Best Practices

### Executive Summary
- Limit to one screen/page
- Use bullet points for key highlights
- Include visual timeline if possible

### Technical Details
- Progressive disclosure of complexity
- Start with high-level concepts before details
- Include diagrams for complex architectural concepts
- Link to external references rather than duplicating content

### Action Items 
- Group related items together
- Make each item atomic and testable
- Include clear "Definition of Done" criteria
- Identify dependencies between items

### Risk Assessment
Use a standard risk matrix format:

```markdown
| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation Strategy |
|------|------------|--------|---------------------|
| [Description] | [H/M/L] | [H/M/L] | [Strategy details] |
```

## Review Checklist
End your implementation plan with a review checklist:

- [ ] All sections complete
- [ ] Technical approach reviewed by engineering team
- [ ] Dependencies identified and documented
- [ ] Effort estimates validated
- [ ] Testing strategy addresses all components
- [ ] Risk assessment includes mitigation strategies
- [ ] Timeline is realistic and accounts for dependencies
- [ ] Resource requirements clearly stated
- [ ] Success criteria defined for each phase
- [ ] Integration points with other systems documented
